# The compositor.
#
# IT RUNS BESIDE THE CONSOLE, NOT INSTEAD OF IT, AND THAT IS THE IMPORTANT
# DECISION. STAGE5.md describes `exec labwc` from /etc/profile, which would
# replace the login shell -- and a compositor that exits then leaves a machine
# with no shell at all. This one WILL exit, repeatedly, while it is being
# brought up: no DRM device, no seat, a broken rc.xml. The serial console has
# to stay the thing that always works, so labwc is its own service and the
# getty keeps running.
#
# restart = true, AND THE ARGUMENT THAT SAID false IS WORTH KEEPING IN VIEW.
# It read: "dinit would otherwise respawn a compositor that cannot start, once
# per second, filling the console with the same error and hiding the boot. One
# attempt, one message, and `dinitctl start labwc` to try again after fixing
# something."
#
# THAT REASONING ASSUMED A PERSON WHO CAN REACH dinitctl, and the assumption
# broke on hardware. Killing labwc to recover from a wedged lock screen left
# `[STOPPD] labwc` and a blank tty1; `dinitctl start labwc` then failed
# because dinitctl defaults to the USER socket, which this system does not
# have. Two documented mechanisms, neither of which worked, and no desktop.
#
# THE CRASH-LOOP FEAR IS HANDLED WITHOUT A SETTING. dinit(8) describes
# "automatic rate-limited restart of failing processes" as built in, so a
# compositor that cannot start is retried at a bounded rate rather than once
# per second. Nothing here configures that, and an earlier attempt to
# configure it invented two setting names dinit does not have --
# restart-limit-count and restart-limit-interval -- which made the service
# description fail to load and took the whole boot with it.
#
# WHAT IT DEPENDS ON, and each is a real failure this ordering prevents:
#   seatd         no session, no DRM device
#   xdg-runtime   labwc exits at src/main.c:242 if XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is unset
#   early-filesystems  /run must be a tmpfs before either of the above
#   hotplug       amdgpu loads at coldplug; its native probe EVICTS
#                 simpledrm, so after hotplug there is exactly one card
#                 and it is the right one. Start before it and the
#                 compositor binds the firmware framebuffer -- and
#                 libudev-zero means it never learns a real GPU arrived.
#
# THE ENVIRONMENT IS SET HERE AND NOT INHERITED. dinit does not source
# /etc/profile -- that is a login shell's job -- so a service started at boot
# gets PID 1's environment, which has neither XDG_RUNTIME_DIR nor a usable
# PATH. The wrapper script sets both.
#
# IN boot.d NOW, AND THE NOTE THAT SAID OTHERWISE IS WORTH KEEPING IN VIEW.
# It read: "Adding the symlink would start a compositor on every boot,
# including the harness boots that exist to run package tests, and a failure
# there would look like a broken system rather than a machine with no display.
# Start it with `dinitctl start labwc`, or add the symlink once the session is
# known to work."
#
# BOTH OF ITS CONDITIONS ARE NOW MET. A harness boot never reaches dinit at
# all -- guest/init runs the suite under the default `veron.boot=test` and
# powers off, and only `veron.boot=system` switch_roots -- so nothing here can
# be reached by a package test. And the session is known to work: it has been
# started by hand on a laptop, repeatedly, since.
#
# THE FALLBACK IS WHAT MAKES IT SAFE, and it is the paragraph above rather
# than anything added here: a console service that runs independently means a
# compositor which cannot start still leaves a shell. That was true when this
# service did not restart and it is true now -- restarting changes how long
# the desktop stays gone, not whether there is a way in.
# run-as = veron IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS SERVICE FILE NOW.
#
# The compositor, the bar, the terminal and the browser are all children of
# this process, so this one word is what moves the entire desktop off uid 0. A
# WebKit bug stops being a whole-machine compromise and becomes one
# unprivileged account -- on a system where the browser is by far the largest
# piece of attack surface.
#
# IT WORKS BECAUSE OF seatd AND NOT IN SPITE OF IT. wlroots needs the DRM
# device and the evdev nodes, and a normal user can open neither; seatd runs
# as root, opens them, and passes the descriptors over /run/seatd.sock. Group
# `video` is what admits us to that socket -- see etc/dinit.d/seatd, which
# runs `seatd -g video`.
#
# THE CONSOLE STAYS ROOT ON PURPOSE. etc/dinit.d/console is a separate service
# and is untouched: if the desktop cannot start, or the user account is
# misconfigured, there must still be a way in. That is the same reasoning that
# made labwc a service beside the console rather than a replacement for it.
type = process
command = /bin/busybox sh /etc/dinit.d/scripts/labwc-session
run-as = veron
restart = true

# logfile SO A FAILURE SAYS WHY. labwc terminated with exit code 1 on the boot
# of run 85938164747 and started fine from dinitctl a moment later; the console
# carried dinit's report of the exit but not a word from labwc itself, because
# its stderr went into the same serial stream as everything else and did not
# survive. With this the reason lands in a file the desktop test can read back.
logfile = /run/labwc.log
depends-on = seatd
depends-on = xdg-runtime
depends-on = early-filesystems
depends-on = device-nodes
depends-on = hotplug
#   session-gate  the HARD boot gate: when armed, this edge is what
#                 makes "no factor, no desktop" structural rather than
#                 a property of any process's lifetime.
depends-on = session-gate
# after splash, but NOT gated on it: splash execs fbsplash and exits, and a
# hard dependency on a restart=false one-shot is a fragile edge. Ordering only
# -- the wallpaper is on the panel before the compositor, but splash failing
# or being absent never stops labwc.
waits-for = splash
